      {
        "id": "p16-q52-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $P(y)=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(y)=y^3-7y^3+6y^3=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\nP(y)&=y^3-7y(y^2)+6y^3\\\\\nP(y)&=y^3-7y^3+6y^3\\\\\nP(y)&=0.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q52-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, factorise $x^3-7xy^2+6y^3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x-y)(x-2y)(x+3y)$.\n\nSince $P(y)=0$, divide by $x-y$, then factorise the quadratic quotient.",
          "worked": "Since $P(y)=0$, the factor theorem gives $x-y$ as a factor. Long division gives\n$$\\begin{array}{r|rrrr}\n&&x^2&+xy&-6y^2\\\\\\hline\nx-y&x^3&+0x^2&-7xy^2&+6y^3\\\\\n&-x^3&+x^2y&&\\\\\\hline\n&&x^2y&-7xy^2&+6y^3\\\\\n&&-x^2y&+xy^2&\\\\\\hline\n&&&-6xy^2&+6y^3\\\\\n&&&+6xy^2&-6y^3\\\\\\hline\n&&&&0\n\\end{array}$$\nFactorise the quadratic quotient:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP(x)&=(x-y)(x^2+xy-6y^2)\\\\\nP(x)&=(x-y)(x-2y)(x+3y).\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 140
      }
    ]
